INT. BOWLING ALLEY SNACK COUNTER - AFTERNOON

A sleepy league day. The pins clatter in the distance like a slow applause. Five people share a booth built for four.

PRISCILLA "PIP" NAKASHIMA (30s, team captain, sunburnt nose) stirs a milkshake with great concentration. Next to her, her husband WALTER GRUNDY-BELL (30s, wearing a shirt that says BOWL OR DIE) eats nachos one chip at a time. Across the table, her brother OTIS (20s, hoodie, headphones) stares into his phone. Squeezed at the end are the Pellegrinos: elderly twins CARMELA and CORRADO PELLEGRINO (70s, matching cardigans), who have been bowling together since the Truman administration.

PIP
Walter, you promised you'd bowl a hundred.

WALTER
I bowled ninety-eight, Pip.

PIP
That's two short.

WALTER
It was a very good ninety-eight.

OTIS
(not looking up)
It was a gutter and a prayer.

CARMELA
Corrado, pass the salt.

CORRADO
Carmela, you don't need salt.

CARMELA
I need what I need.

Corrado slides the shaker across the table. Carmela and Walter both reach for it at the same time and knock the nachos into Pip's lap. All five freeze.

PIP
(with great calm)
Walter.

WALTER
I'll get napkins.

CORRADO
Carmela, you did that.

CARMELA
I did not.

Otis finally lifts his eyes from the phone, examines the carnage, and returns to the screen without a word. Pip dabs cheese from her knee and takes a slow, controlled sip of milkshake.

PIP
Next frame, Walter. Hundred or nothing.
